  <data name="OnexCaption669.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Replaces the legacy ViGEm controller emulation with VIIPER (USBIP-based). Requires usbip-win2. Toggling restarts controller emulation.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="OnexCaption670.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>usbip-win2 driver: checking...</value>
  </data>
  <data name="OnexCaption671.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>⚠ usbip-win2 required</value>
  </data>
  <data name="OnexCaption672.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>VIIPER emulation needs the usbip-win2 kernel driver. Download + install the signed driver, reboot, then come back here.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="OnexCaption673.Content" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Install usbip-win2</value>
  </data>
  <data name="OnexCaption674.Content" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Or open usbip-win2 releases</value>
  </data>
  <data name="OnexCaption675.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Fan curve: allow 1–39 %</value>
  </data>
  <data name="OnexCaption676.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The custom fan curve normally only rests at 0 (off) or 40 % and above, and the graph is drawn with a break across that gap. The reason: on both the Claw 8 AI+ and the Claw 8 EX the fan sits on its minimum-spin plateau below ~40, so 20 and 40 measured the same RPM — a slider that promises a difference it cannot deliver.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="OnexCaption677.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Turn this on to lift the restriction: every value 1–39 becomes settable and the axis is drawn straight. Nothing about the EC write changes — those values were always sent as-is. Switching it back off snaps the curve onto the allowed values again.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="OnexCaption678.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Per-game gyro engine</value>
  </data>
  <data name="OnexCaption679.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Which engine a per-game controller profile's gyro uses. Auto reads the sensor over the COM Sensor API — the one path that works on the Claw 8 AI+ and the Claw 8 EX alike, confirmed on real hardware on both. If the Sensor API finds nothing, Auto falls back to the WinRT sensor by itself. Applies immediately.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="OnexCaption680.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The other entries are for testing: 'Virtual (software)' forces the WinRT sensor (which finds nothing on the EX, whose gyro reports a different type ID), 'Firmware (SensorModule)' leaves motion to the controller itself, and 'Virtual (Sensor API)' pins the COM source with no fallback.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="OnexCaption681.Content" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Auto (Sensor API)</value>
  </data>
